    {
      "id": "q001",
      "title": "Inverse FT and inverse DTFT using properties",
      "type": "inverse_transform",
      "chapter": "3",
      "sections": [
        "3.6",
        "3.7",
        "3.11",
        "3.12",
        "3.13",
        "3.15"
      ],
      "difficulty": "hard",
      "prompt": "(a) Using multiple properties to find an Inverse FT. Find x(t) if X(jω)=j d/dω { e^{j2ω}/(1+j(ω/3)) }.\n(b) Find the inverse DTFT of X(e^{jΩ})=(-5/6 e^{-jΩ}+5)/(1+1/6 e^{-jΩ}-1/6 e^{-j2Ω}).",
      "latex": {
        "a": "X(j\\omega)=j\\frac{d}{d\\omega}\\left\\{\\frac{e^{j2\\omega}}{1+j(\\omega/3)}\\right\\}",
        "b": "X(e^{j\\Omega})=\\frac{-\\frac56 e^{-j\\Omega}+5}{1+\\frac16 e^{-j\\Omega}-\\frac16 e^{-j2\\Omega}}"
      },
      "final_answer": {
        "a": "x(t)=3t e^{-3(t+2)}u(t+2)",
        "b": "x[n]=\\left(\\frac13\\right)^n u[n]+4\\left(-\\frac12\\right)^n u[n]"
      },
      "solution_steps": [
        {
          "part": "a",
          "step": 1,
          "text": "Let s(t)=e^{-t}u(t), so S(jω)=1/(1+jω)."
        },
        {
          "part": "a",
          "step": 2,
          "text": "1/(1+j(ω/3)) = S(jω/3). By scaling, S(jω/3) ↔ 3s(3t)=3e^{-3t}u(t)."
        },
        {
          "part": "a",
          "step": 3,
          "text": "Multiplying by e^{j2ω}=e^{-jω(-2)} shifts time by +2 in the argument: e^{j2ω}S(jω/3) ↔ 3e^{-3(t+2)}u(t+2)."
        },
        {
          "part": "a",
          "step": 4,
          "text": "The outer factor j d/dω in frequency corresponds to multiplying by t in time."
        },
        {
          "part": "a",
          "step": 5,
          "text": "Therefore x(t)=3t e^{-3(t+2)}u(t+2)."
        },
        {
          "part": "b",
          "step": 1,
          "text": "Set q=e^{-jΩ}. Then X=(5-(5/6)q)/(1+(1/6)q-(1/6)q^2)."
        },
        {
          "part": "b",
          "step": 2,
          "text": "Factor the denominator: 1+(1/6)q-(1/6)q^2=(1-(1/3)q)(1+(1/2)q)."
        },
        {
          "part": "b",
          "step": 3,
          "text": "Use partial fractions: X=A/(1-(1/3)q)+B/(1+(1/2)q)."
        },
        {
          "part": "b",
          "step": 4,
          "text": "Compare coefficients: A+B=5 and (1/2)A-(1/3)B=-5/6. Thus A=1, B=4."
        },
        {
          "part": "b",
          "step": 5,
          "text": "So X=1/(1-(1/3)e^{-jΩ})+4/(1-(-1/2)e^{-jΩ})."
        },
        {
          "part": "b",
          "step": 6,
          "text": "Use a^n u[n] ↔ 1/(1-ae^{-jΩ})."
        }
      ],
      "key_formulas": [
        "e^{-at}u(t)\\leftrightarrow \\frac1{a+j\\omega}",
        "X(j\\omega/a)\\leftrightarrow |a|x(at)",
        "e^{-j\\omega t_0}X(j\\omega)\\leftrightarrow x(t-t_0)",
        "tx(t)\\leftrightarrow j\\frac{d}{d\\omega}X(j\\omega)",
        "a^n u[n]\\leftrightarrow \\frac1{1-ae^{-j\\Omega}}"
      ],
      "tags": [
        "FT",
        "DTFT",
        "time shift",
        "scaling",
        "partial fractions"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "q002",
      "title": "LTI frequency response and impulse response",
      "type": "system_response",
      "chapter": "2/3",
      "sections": [
        "LTI systems",
        "3.6",
        "3.7",
        "3.13"
      ],
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "prompt": "(a) The output of an LTI system in response to x(t)=e^{-2t}u(t) is y(t)=e^{-t}u(t). Find the frequency response and impulse response.\n(b) Find the frequency response and impulse response of the discrete-time system 20y[n]-9y[n-1]+y[n-2]=100x[n]-23x[n-1].",
      "latex": {
        "a": "x(t)=e^{-2t}u(t),\\quad y(t)=e^{-t}u(t)",
        "b": "20y[n]-9y[n-1]+y[n-2]=100x[n]-23x[n-1]"
      },
      "final_answer": {
        "a": "H(j\\omega)=\\frac{j\\omega+2}{j\\omega+1},\\quad h(t)=\\delta(t)+e^{-t}u(t)",
        "b": "H(e^{j\\Omega})=\\frac{100-23e^{-j\\Omega}}{20-9e^{-j\\Omega}+e^{-j2\\Omega}}=\\frac{2}{1-\\frac14e^{-j\\Omega}}+\\frac{3}{1-\\frac15e^{-j\\Omega}},\\quad h[n]=2(\\frac14)^n u[n]+3(\\frac15)^n u[n]"
      },
      "solution_steps": [
        {
          "part": "a",
          "step": 1,
          "text": "Transform input and output: X(jω)=1/(2+jω), Y(jω)=1/(1+jω)."
        },
        {
          "part": "a",
          "step": 2,
          "text": "For an LTI system, Y=HX, so H=Y/X=(jω+2)/(jω+1)."
        },
        {
          "part": "a",
          "step": 3,
          "text": "Rewrite H=(jω+2)/(jω+1)=1+1/(jω+1)."
        },
        {
          "part": "a",
          "step": 4,
          "text": "Use 1 ↔ δ(t) and 1/(jω+1) ↔ e^{-t}u(t)."
        },
        {
          "part": "b",
          "step": 1,
          "text": "Apply DTFT to the difference equation: y[n-k] ↔ e^{-jΩk}Y(e^{jΩ})."
        },
        {
          "part": "b",
          "step": 2,
          "text": "Get (20-9e^{-jΩ}+e^{-j2Ω})Y=(100-23e^{-jΩ})X."
        },
        {
          "part": "b",
          "step": 3,
          "text": "Therefore H=Y/X=(100-23e^{-jΩ})/(20-9e^{-jΩ}+e^{-j2Ω})."
        },
        {
          "part": "b",
          "step": 4,
          "text": "Let q=e^{-jΩ}. Factor denominator: 20-9q+q^2=20(1-q/4)(1-q/5)."
        },
        {
          "part": "b",
          "step": 5,
          "text": "Partial fraction: H=2/(1-q/4)+3/(1-q/5)."
        },
        {
          "part": "b",
          "step": 6,
          "text": "Use inverse DTFT a^n u[n] ↔ 1/(1-ae^{-jΩ})."
        }
      ],
      "key_formulas": [
        "Y=HX",
        "H=Y/X",
        "e^{-at}u(t)\\leftrightarrow \\frac1{a+j\\omega}",
        "a^n u[n]\\leftrightarrow \\frac1{1-ae^{-j\\Omega}}",
        "x[n-k]\\leftrightarrow e^{-j\\Omega k}X(e^{j\\Omega})"
      ],
      "tags": [
        "LTI",
        "frequency response",
        "impulse response"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "q003",
      "title": "FT using time shift, convolution, and differentiation",
      "type": "fourier_properties",
      "chapter": "3",
      "sections": [
        "3.10",
        "3.11",
        "3.12"
      ],
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "prompt": "Use the properties of Fourier representation, e.g., time-differentiation, convolution, time-shift, frequency-shift, to find the FT of y(t)=d/dt{e^{-4t}u(t-5)*e^{-2t}u(t-3)}. Note: * denotes convolution.",
      "latex": {
        "main": "y(t)=\\frac{d}{dt}\\{e^{-4t}u(t-5)*e^{-2t}u(t-3)\\}"
      },
      "final_answer": {
        "main": "Y(j\\omega)=\\frac{j\\omega e^{-26}e^{-j8\\omega}}{(4+j\\omega)(2+j\\omega)}"
      },
      "solution_steps": [
        {
          "step": 1,
          "text": "Rewrite e^{-4t}u(t-5)=e^{-20}e^{-4(t-5)}u(t-5). Thus A(jω)=e^{-20}e^{-j5ω}/(4+jω)."
        },
        {
          "step": 2,
          "text": "Rewrite e^{-2t}u(t-3)=e^{-6}e^{-2(t-3)}u(t-3). Thus B(jω)=e^{-6}e^{-j3ω}/(2+jω)."
        },
        {
          "step": 3,
          "text": "Convolution in time becomes multiplication in frequency: A(jω)B(jω)=e^{-26}e^{-j8ω}/((4+jω)(2+jω))."
        },
        {
          "step": 4,
          "text": "The outer time derivative corresponds to multiplying by jω."
        }
      ],
      "key_formulas": [
        "e^{-at}u(t-t_0)\\leftrightarrow \\frac{e^{-at_0}e^{-j\\omega t_0}}{a+j\\omega}",
        "x(t)*h(t)\\leftrightarrow X(j\\omega)H(j\\omega)",
        "\\frac{d}{dt}x(t)\\leftrightarrow j\\omega X(j\\omega)"
      ],
      "tags": [
        "time shift",
        "convolution",
        "differentiation"
      ]
    },
    {
